1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,968
Four different AI models are about to look at my handwriting, and they are going to vote on what I wrote.

2
00:00:05,248 --> 00:00:10,192
That blurry blob is what they actually see: an eight by eight grid of pixels.

3
00:00:10,472 --> 00:00:12,344
Sixty-four numbers.

4
00:00:12,624 --> 00:00:20,448
From that, a support vector machine, a small neural network, a random forest and a nearest-neighbour model each give an answer.

5
00:00:20,728 --> 00:00:22,600
Most of the time they agree.

6
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:25,832
Sometimes they don't, and that is where it gets interesting.

7
00:00:26,112 --> 00:00:33,024
I'm going to show you how I built this, and why a few lines of unglamorous preprocessing mattered more than the choice of model.

8
00:00:33,824 --> 00:00:40,232
Quick honesty first: I built this with Claude Code, and the narration you're hearing is AI-generated.

9
00:00:40,512 --> 00:00:44,640
The code, though, is real and short, and I'll show it.

10
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:49,408
Why does a digit classifier matter in a world of giant language models?

11
00:00:49,688 --> 00:00:54,128
Because it is the cleanest place to understand what machine learning actually is.

12
00:00:54,408 --> 00:00:56,304
You have examples with labels.

13
00:00:56,584 --> 00:00:58,456
You pick a model.

14
00:00:58,736 --> 00:01:00,608
You fit it.

15
00:01:00,888 --> 00:01:03,360
You test it on data it has never seen.

16
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:08,680
That loop is the same for the biggest models in the world; it is just easier to see here.

17
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:19,064
The dataset is the scikit-learn digits set: one thousand seven hundred and ninety-seven images, each eight by eight pixels, with values from zero to sixteen.

18
00:01:19,344 --> 00:01:21,576
That is tiny, which is the point.

19
00:01:21,856 --> 00:01:28,696
The app trains four models when it starts, on a laptop CPU, with no GPU and no API calls.

20
00:01:28,976 --> 00:01:32,192
There is also a trap hiding in benchmark numbers.

21
00:01:32,472 --> 00:01:37,920
A model that scores ninety-nine percent on a test split can still stumble on your own handwriting.

22
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:41,368
Understanding why is the real lesson of this video.

23
00:01:42,167 --> 00:01:46,631
The app is one file, about a hundred and twenty lines, in Streamlit.

24
00:01:46,911 --> 00:01:53,799
Three pieces matter: training, a bit of augmentation, and converting your drawing into something the models can read.

25
00:01:54,079 --> 00:01:55,951
First, training.

26
00:01:56,231 --> 00:01:59,807
This is the function, cached so it only runs once.

27
00:02:00,087 --> 00:02:04,911
We hold out a quarter of the images, four hundred and fifty of them, as a test set.

28
00:02:05,191 --> 00:02:09,871
Stratify means each digit appears in the same proportion in both halves.

29
00:02:10,151 --> 00:02:14,759
Then four models, all from scikit-learn, all trained on the same data.

30
00:02:15,039 --> 00:02:20,799
The accuracy table in the app is computed on that held-out quarter, never on the training images.

31
00:02:21,079 --> 00:02:22,975
Second, augmentation.

32
00:02:23,255 --> 00:02:30,167
Hand-drawn digits are not perfectly centred, so I shift every training image by one pixel in four directions.

33
00:02:30,447 --> 00:02:37,239
That turns one thousand three hundred and forty-seven training images into six thousand seven hundred and thirty-five.

34
00:02:37,519 --> 00:02:43,975
Five times the data, for free, and the models become less sensitive to exactly where you put the digit.

35
00:02:44,255 --> 00:02:47,663
Third, the bridge between your mouse and the model.

36
00:02:47,943 --> 00:02:54,951
The browser canvas is downsampled to fifty-six by fifty-six, and then Python reduces that to eight by eight.

37
00:02:55,231 --> 00:02:57,103
Read it top to bottom.

38
00:02:57,383 --> 00:02:59,255
Find where the ink is.

39
00:02:59,535 --> 00:03:01,407
Crop to the bounding box.

40
00:03:01,687 --> 00:03:04,567
Scale so the longer side is eight pixels.

41
00:03:04,847 --> 00:03:07,151
Centre it in an eight by eight box.

42
00:03:07,431 --> 00:03:11,535
Then stretch the brightness to the dataset's zero to sixteen range.

43
00:03:11,815 --> 00:03:15,655
Each step exists to make my drawing look like the training images.

44
00:03:15,935 --> 00:03:17,807
Finally the vote.

45
00:03:18,087 --> 00:03:25,575
Each model gives probabilities for the ten digits, we take each model's top pick, and the most common answer is the majority vote.

46
00:03:25,855 --> 00:03:27,727
Three lines.

47
00:03:28,007 --> 00:03:29,879
That's an ensemble.

48
00:03:30,159 --> 00:03:37,959
The drawing canvas itself is a small inline Streamlit component written in JavaScript, so there is no extra package to install.

49
00:03:38,239 --> 00:03:41,839
When you lift the mouse, it sends the pixels back to Python.

50
00:03:42,639 --> 00:03:44,511
Here's the app.

51
00:03:44,791 --> 00:03:48,079
On the left, the canvas and the accuracy table.

52
00:03:48,359 --> 00:04:03,959
On unseen data, when I ran the same split and augmentation headlessly, the SVM scored ninety-nine point eight percent, k-nearest-neighbours ninety-eight point four, the random forest ninety-eight point two, and the neural net ninety-eight point zero.

53
00:04:04,239 --> 00:04:08,007
So all four are in the ninety-eight to ninety-nine percent range.

54
00:04:08,287 --> 00:04:10,159
Let me draw a seven.

55
00:04:10,439 --> 00:04:15,263
Big, one digit, like a dot-matrix display, as the tip says.

56
00:04:15,543 --> 00:04:22,623
There's the eight by eight view, the majority vote, and on the right a bar chart of each model's probability for each digit.

57
00:04:22,903 --> 00:04:30,823
On this drawing the SVM and k-nearest-neighbours both said seven, while the neural net and the random forest both said five.

58
00:04:31,103 --> 00:04:34,223
That is a two-two split, and the app showed seven.

59
00:04:34,503 --> 00:04:38,007
Keep that in mind, because I come back to it in the limits.

60
00:04:38,287 --> 00:04:40,159
Now a three.

61
00:04:40,439 --> 00:04:44,255
The SVM, the forest and k-nearest-neighbours say three.

62
00:04:44,535 --> 00:04:47,631
The neural net says eight, with near certainty.

63
00:04:47,911 --> 00:04:52,615
The majority wins, but the dissenter was confident, and that is worth noticing.

64
00:04:52,895 --> 00:04:56,711
Now let me be mean and draw a messy four with an open top.

65
00:04:56,991 --> 00:05:00,519
The SVM, the neural net and the forest say four.

66
00:05:00,799 --> 00:05:02,719
k-nearest-neighbours says one.

67
00:05:02,999 --> 00:05:08,255
The majority vote gives one answer, but the chart tells you how confident each model was.

68
00:05:08,535 --> 00:05:12,519
That's the difference between a prediction and a prediction you should trust.

69
00:05:12,799 --> 00:05:19,063
The cropping step enlarges any digit to fill the box, so size matters little, but shape does.

70
00:05:19,863 --> 00:05:21,735
Three things.

71
00:05:22,015 --> 00:05:25,327
One: the preprocessing mattered more than the model.

72
00:05:25,607 --> 00:05:34,391
A comment in the code says it directly: centring by centre of mass hurt top-heavy digits like seven, so I centre by the bounding box instead.

73
00:05:34,671 --> 00:05:39,447
That's the kind of thing you only learn by drawing digits and watching them get misread.

74
00:05:39,727 --> 00:05:42,319
Two: simple models are very good here.

75
00:05:42,599 --> 00:05:50,951
A k-nearest-neighbours classifier, which essentially remembers the training images and looks for the closest ones, scores about ninety-eight percent.

76
00:05:51,231 --> 00:05:54,711
A neural network is not better on a dataset this small.

77
00:05:54,991 --> 00:06:00,031
Three: the accuracy table is a statement about the test set, not about you.

78
00:06:00,311 --> 00:06:06,527
All four models score high on dataset images, but your hand-drawn digit comes from a different distribution.

79
00:06:06,807 --> 00:06:10,239
Augmentation narrows that gap; it does not close it.

80
00:06:11,039 --> 00:06:13,247
Let me be straight about what this is not.

81
00:06:13,527 --> 00:06:18,735
It is eight by eight pixels, so it is a toy compared with real handwriting recognition.

82
00:06:19,015 --> 00:06:26,311
Some drawings will be misread, especially digits with unusual shapes or strokes that blur together at that resolution.

83
00:06:26,591 --> 00:06:37,223
I have not measured accuracy on my own drawings across many samples, so I won't claim a number for it; the ninety-eight to ninety-nine percent figure is for the held-out part of the dataset only.

84
00:06:37,503 --> 00:06:43,335
The majority vote has no sophisticated tie-break, so a two-two split is close to arbitrary.

85
00:06:43,615 --> 00:06:48,751
And the models use a fixed random seed, so you're seeing one run, not an average.

86
00:06:49,031 --> 00:06:51,575
I also built this with an AI assistant.

87
00:06:51,855 --> 00:06:56,631
I ran the code and checked the numbers, but you should read it yourself before trusting it.

88
00:06:57,431 --> 00:07:03,407
If you want to take this further: swap the classic models for a small convolutional network and compare.

89
00:07:03,687 --> 00:07:06,831
There is a CNN project in the same repository.

90
00:07:07,111 --> 00:07:14,335
You could also collect your own drawings, label them, and measure real accuracy, which would be a more honest benchmark than the table.

91
